This project (Orin) is a CI/CD tool for automating update server-side's services.

It requires a valid JDK installation(JDK 22 or above) to run, and in most cases, a valid network port is required for Github webhooks.

Current minimal workflow support:
- `POST /webhook` or `POST /webhook/github` accepts an authenticated GitHub push payload and queues matching pipelines.
- `POST /trigger/{pipeline}` triggers a configured pipeline manually. Send the configured token and optional inputs:
  `{"token":"the-manual-token","inputs":{"branch":"main"}}`
- Pipelines reference actions by name, and each action is loaded from `actions/<name>/entry.toml`.
- Action entry can be either `.kts` or `.sh`.
- KTS actions receive a selectively exposed read-only `context` plus a small `orin` API. Actions are trusted CI code and are not sandboxed.
- Pipeline execution uses a bounded single-worker queue so actions never concurrently mutate the shared workspace.
- Action processes have configurable execution time and captured-output limits.

Configure both authentication secrets directly in `config.toml`:

```toml
[Webhook]
githubSecret = "the-secret-configured-in-github"

[[Pipeline]]
manualToken = "a-separate-manual-trigger-token"
```

The configuration contains plaintext credentials. Limit its filesystem access and
repository visibility accordingly. Orin refuses to start when an enabled trigger
does not have its required secret configured.

GitHub deliveries must include `X-GitHub-Event: push` and a valid
`X-Hub-Signature-256: sha256=<hmac>` header. Orin returns `202 Accepted` after the
matching pipeline has entered the runner queue. A full queue returns `503` so
GitHub can retry the delivery.

`[Runner]` controls the bounded queue and process limits:

```toml
[Runner]
queueCapacity = 100
actionTimeoutSeconds = 900
maxOutputBytes = 1048576
```

Manual triggers run through the same queue and wait for completion. A full queue
returns HTTP `429`.

Example action layout:
`actions/github-push-summary/entry.toml`
`actions/github-push-summary/main.kts`
`actions/build-shell/entry.toml`
`actions/build-shell/main.sh`

For it's old GoLang implementation, see https://github.com/glowingstone124/GoCi.

Its name was inspired from Kaenbyou Rin, a character from Touhou Project.

